# The five markers: each
# is the identity on what it wraps, spelled as a one-key partial function of a
# literal, of the identity, applied to that literal, and then to the wrapped
# term: (λ"$k". λv. v) "$k" is λv. v by the β rule (a partial function of a
# literal applied to that literal reads its bits one by one), so the marker
# applied to M is M. The key's match is a closed term, the same at every use,
# so a tabled evaluator matches it once per reading state and each further
# pass costs 6 steps more than an identity's (measured 2026-11-17). Every
# reader of these markers is tabled: weak head without tabling is a rejected
# design. The keys start with `$`, synthetic labels, which the compiler's
# knowledge writes and an end user's program does not: lemmas are for the
# compiler, not for end users.
# $generic:
# The marker of a polymorphic library function: the function's class holds
# its scheme alone, and the closure under the marker is a class of its own;
# noinline.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λx. (λ"$generic" v. v) "$generic" x)
λ $generic
# $constructor:
# The marker of a constructor; noinline.
. (λtuple. tuple λx. (λ"$constructor" v. v) "$constructor" x)
λ $constructor
# $name:
# The occurrence t of a library name x in a program: $name t x is x, the tag t
# unique to the occurrence, so that hash consing makes no two occurrences of
# the program one class ($closure for a closure's); noinline. Evaluation
# reads x and never reads t.
. (λtuple. tuple λt x. (λ"$name" v. v) "$name" x)
λ $name
# $closure:
# The occurrence t of a closure c in a program: $closure t c is c, the tag t
# unique to the occurrence, as $name is for the occurrence of a library name;
# noinline. Evaluation reads c and never reads t.
. (λtuple. tuple λt c. (λ"$closure" v. v) "$closure" c)
λ $closure
# $repelled:
# The marker of a term the extraction does not spell: a class that holds
# $repelled applied to a term has no entry, so no term the extraction spells
# reads through it (the combinator property repeller); noinline. The
# knowledge writes it at a typed version's filter whose operand's type is
# refuted for the filter.
. (λtuple. tuple λx. (λ"$repelled" v. v) "$repelled" x)
λ $repelled
. export $generic $constructor $name $closure $repelled
